2,0.246,0.263,0.099,0.049,0.084,0.1,0.073,0.054,0.227,0.08,0.174,0.164,0.08,0.191,0.099,0.021,0.031,0.025,0.093,0.11,0.19,0.088,0.087,0.119,0.131,0.206,0.169,0.208,0.138,0.119,0.211,0.099,0.137,0.019,0.071,0.065,0.017,0.012,0.114,0.045,0.033,0.031,0.137,0.1,0.052,0.014,0.139,0.06,0.074,0.128,0.054,0.021,0.032,0.046,0.109,0.07,0.047,0.03,0.01,0.004,0.077,0.211,0.062,0.075,0.089,0.14,0.092,0.043,0.014,0.022,0.005,0,0.039,0.125,0.087,0.084,0.027,0.084,0.06,0.093,0.144,0.059,0.128,0.204,0.046,0.049,0.034,0.029,0.05,0.247,0.279,0.018,0.103,0.062,0.011,0.13,0.052,0.107,0.091,0.1,0.139,0.132,0.074,0.143,0.13,0.081,0.008,0.104,0.143,0.039,0.014,0.266,0.156,0.036]}},"alerts":[{"event":"Flood Watch","headline":"Flood Watch issued September 30 at 12:33AM MDT until October 1 at 6:00AM MDT by NWS Pueblo CO","description":"* WHAT...Flash flooding caused by excessive rainfall continues to be\npossible.\n\n* WHERE...The Eastern San Juan Mountains.\n\n* WHEN...Through late tonight.\n\n* IMPACTS...Excessive runoff may result in flooding of rivers,\ncreeks, streams, and other low-lying and flood-prone locations.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- Periods of heavy rainfall associated with a remnant tropical\nstorm system will spread across southwest Colorado through\nWednesday night.\n- Creede, South Fork and Wolf Creek Pass.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-10-01T06:00:00-06:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Upper Rio Grande Valley/Eastern San Juan Mountains Below 10000 Ft; Eastern San Juan Mountains Above 10000 Ft","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.9dbc646753cca47964bbc82b5c90e03af46eea35.003.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.9dbc646753cca47964bbc82b5c90e03af46eea35.003.1"},{"event":"Flood Watch","headline":"Flood Watch issued September 29 at 10:07PM MDT until October 1 at 6:00AM MDT by NWS Grand Junction CO","description":"* WHAT...Flooding caused by excessive rainfall continues to be\npossible.\n\n* WHERE...Portions of Colorado, including the following areas, Animas\nRiver Basin, Four Corners/Upper Dolores River, Northwest San Juan\nMountains, Paradox Valley/Lower Dolores River, San Juan River\nBasin, Southwest San Juan Mountains and Uncompahgre Plateau and\nDallas Divide and southeast Utah, including the following areas,\nCanyonlands/Natural Bridges, La Sal and Abajo Mountains and\nSoutheast Utah.\n\n* WHEN...Through late Wednesday night.\n\n* IMPACTS...Flooding will be possible over burn scars, at low-water\ncrossings, and along creeks, streams, washes, arroyos and other\nflood-prone locations. Creeks and streams may rise out of their\nbanks. Unpaved roads could become muddy and impassable.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- Periods of heavy rainfall, associated with remnant moisture from\nHurricane Polo, are expected to produce numerous showers and\nembedded thunderstorms. Heavy rain falling on saturated soils and\nburn scars could lead to localized flash flooding and rises on\nlocal rivers. Rainfall is expected to end by late Wednesday, but\nrivers may rise out of their banks overnight Wednesday even after\nrainfall has ended.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-10-01T06:00:00-06:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Northwestern San Juan Mountains; Southwest San Juan Mountains; San Juan River Basin","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.0131f538810dac521ad09e37656bcfc83b15cbff.002.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.0131f538810dac521ad09e37656bcfc83b15cbff.002.1"},{"event":"Hydrologic Outlook","headline":"Hydrologic Outlook issued September 25 at 2:21PM MDT by NWS Grand Junction CO","description":"ESFGJT\n\nIn the wake of today's precipitation, our attention will turn to the\nEastern Pacific. Tropical moisture is anticipated to spread across\nthe Desert Southwest early next week, drawing moisture from a pair of\nHurricanes; Odalys and Polo. The forecast becomes increasing complex\ndue to a closed low currently forecast to drive across the Southwest\nCONUS as Hurricane Polo approaches the Baja California Peninsula.\nStrong southerly winds ahead of the low are expected to pull tropical\nmoisture towards eastern Utah and western Colorado as early as Monday\nmorning. The challenge and uncertainty in our precipitation forecast\nincrease as the week progresses and the low traverses our southern\nStates. Excessive rainfall is most probable over AZ and NM, attm, but\nthe northerly extent poses a threat to our southern San Juan river\nbasins. Current forecasts suggests 1 to 3 inches of rain over Monday\nmorning through Wednesday. At face value, rivers and tributaries will\nsee a bump up due to said moisture. However, localized heavier\namounts could result in minor flooding across the southern San Juan\nfoothills and communities further downstream.\n\nRivers have responded rapidly to recent rainfall due to saturated\nsoils and increased runoff potential. Thus, prolonged periods of\nadditional rainfall over these regions would trigger a similar\nresponse. The current precipitation forecast suggests larger peaks in\nseveral creeks and rivers than what has been observed over the\nprevious 7 days. However, high end probabilistic rainfall amounts\n(i.e. above 90th percentile) may lead to rivers exceeding action\nstage to minor flooding. Most probable basins to see significant\nrises include; the Rio Blanco, San Juan, Piedra, Los Pinos/Vallecito\nCreek, and the Animas River Basins.\n\nConvective storms and periods of heavy rainfall fed from said\ntropical moisture could result in localized flooding impacts across\nthe CWA, however, confidence decreases rapidly north of the San\nJuans. Please continue to monitor the forecast as these systems\nevolve. Especially for those who reside and/or plan to recreate in\nthe southwest quadrant of the forecast area.","ends":"2026-09-30T06:00:00-06:00","severity":"Unknown","areaDesc":"Archuleta, CO; Dolores, CO; Gunnison, CO; Hinsdale, CO; La Plata, CO; Montezuma, CO; Ouray, CO; San Juan, CO; San Miguel, CO; Grand, UT; San Juan, UT","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.ce67801ec73048f07c44dd0418c6a50edd1c8fdc.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.ce67801ec73048f07c44dd0418c6a50edd1c8fdc.001.1"}],"afd":{"office":"GJT","issued":"2026-09-30T11:21:00+00:00","snippet":"000 FXUS65 KGJT 301121 AFDGJT Area Forecast Discussion National Weather Service Grand Junction CO 521 AM MDT Wed Sep 30 2026 .KEY MESSAGES... - Scattered to widespread precipitation showers and storms continue through Wednesday evening. - Flooding concerns are increased for rain-soaked terrain, especially within and adjacent to the southern San Juans. - A Flood Watch is in effect for southeast Uta","url":"https://forecast.weather.gov/product.php?site=GJT&issuedby=GJT&product=AFD&format=CI"},"hwo":null,"fwf":{"office":"GJT","issued":"2026-09-30T07:02:00+00:00","snippet":".DISCUSSION...Moisture will continue to wrap into southern and central portions of the area today as this system moves through. Tropical moisture remains in place and moderate to heavy rain showers will continue to be a threat with high probability of wetting rainfall. Warmer and drier conditions move in by tomorrow and persist through the remainder of the forecast. Critical fire weather condition","url":"https://forecast.weather.gov/product.php?site=GJT&issuedby=GJT&product=FWF&format=CI"},"coagmet":null,"aviation":{"icao":"KAEJ","distance_km":0,"raw_metar":"METAR KAEJ 301135Z AUTO 26004KT 10SM SCT024 BKN065 OVC090 10/08 A3005 RMK AO2 T01010080","temp_f":50,"wind_dir":260,"wind_kt":4,"gust_kt":null,"visibility":"10+","cover":"OVC","flight_category":"VFR","altimeter":1017.7,"observed":1790768100,"raw_taf":null,"url":"https://aviationweather.gov/data/metar/?ids=KAEJ&hours=0"},"airgradient":null,"airnow":{"aqi":9,"category":"Good","parameter":"PM2.5","reporting_area":"Telluride","observed":"2026-09-30 4","by_parameter":{"PM2.5":{"aqi":9,"category":"Good","reporting_area":"Telluride","observed":"2026-09-30 4"},"PM10":{"aqi":